  "query_preview": "..(Tuesday night through Sunday)\nIssued at 330 AM EDT MON OCT 19 2026\n\nThe forecast remains on track, with models and ensembles in good\nagreement through the work week. Broad upper-level ridging over the\ncentral CONUS and robust surface high pressure south of James Bay\nwill maintain tranquil weather across eastern Kentucky. Expect\nrain-free days, mostly clear skies, and a gradual day-to-day\nmoderating trend in temperatures. Toward next weekend, an\nupper-level trough progressing east across Canada may begin to\nsuppress the ridge and displace the surface high eastward.\n\nPrevious Discussion...\n\nThe long-term 18/12Z model suite beginning Tuesday morning is in\ngood agreement, showing broad upper-level ridging (the dominant\ncontrolling weather feature for the long term) over the heart of\nthe CONUS with its ridge axis roughly aligned with the\nMississippi River. This continues to support robust surface\nhigh pressure sprawling across much of eastern North America,\ncentered on a roughly 1035 mb high situated south of James Bay.\nNorth to northeasterly low-level flow will continue to filter\ndown the Appalachians along the eastern side of the high\nthrough the new work week. Uncertainty increases next weekend\nregarding the ridges continued maintenance as an upper-level\ntrough may eject eastward from the Pacific and break it down,\nthough a slim majority of the 00Z LREF members still favor the\nridge holding firm into the last week of October.\n\nIn sensible terms, this persistent surface ridging and\nnortherly to easterly flow will keep fair and seasonably cool\nweather across all of eastern Kentucky with only a slow\nmoderating trend. Highs on Tuesday are forecast to range from\nthe mid 60s to lower 70s, moderating into the upper 60s to mid\n70s by Sunday. Meanwhile, nighttime lows start off in the mid\n30s to lower 40s on Tuesday night and only moderate slightly to\nthe upper 30s to mid 40s by the weekend. Lingering patchy\nfrost is probable Tuesday night into early Wednesday for the\nsheltered valleys, and additional isolated patchy frost cannot\nbe entirely ruled out later in the week in the coldest\nsheltered hollows. Otherwise, at least patchy fog is likely\nalong the mainstem rivers and larger tributaries on most\nnights.",
